Upstream projects
-----------------

Osprey is derived from projects by the flock contributors, all under the MIT
License and Copyright (c) 2026 flock contributors:

  Cuckoo       https://github.com/rjmotion/cuckoo
               A UniFi controller stand-in. The osprey/ controller is that
               code, renamed and extended.
  pyunifiwire  https://github.com/rjmotion/pyunifiwire
               The UniFi wire protocol library, in pyunifiwire/.
  Finch        https://github.com/rjmotion/finch
               A synthetic UniFi camera for testing. It was part of this
               repository's early history, and no Finch source directory
               remains. Any code Osprey carries over from it is covered by the
               same notice.

The copyright notice for all three is kept in LICENSE and in
pyunifiwire/LICENSE. The bundled web libraries have their own notices,
described below.

Interoperability and non-affiliation
------------------------------------

Osprey and pyunifiwire are independent projects. They are not affiliated
with, endorsed by, sponsored by, or supported by Ubiquiti Inc. "Ubiquiti",
"UniFi", "UniFi Protect" and "G5" are trademarks of Ubiquiti Inc. and are used
here only to identify the hardware that Osprey interoperates with. "Frigate"
and "Home Assistant" are the names of other projects, used to say what Osprey
works with.

The UniFi camera wire protocol implemented in pyunifiwire/ was documented by
observing traffic between devices that the authors own, for the sole purpose
of interoperability. No Ubiquiti firmware, software or other copyrighted
material is distributed with Osprey. Protocol behaviour can change with any
camera firmware update; use Osprey at your own risk, and only with cameras you
own or are authorised to manage.

The operator console bundles preact and @preact/signals (MIT) and htm
(Apache-2.0). Their license texts are in osprey/web/vendor/.

Osprey does not bundle Frigate, FFmpeg, OpenSSL or Python. Container images
built from docker/Dockerfile include Debian packages (including FFmpeg and
OpenSSL) and paho-mqtt under their own licenses; the image SBOM published with
each release lists them.

osprey/third_party/raptor_vision is a copy of the core of raptor-vision
(https://github.com/grayslawson/raptor-vision), MIT licence, by the same author.
Its licence text is in that directory.
